{
  "summary": "This 20-second clip captures the opening procedural exchange of an IELTS speaking test. Examiner Mark Reed introduces the test and asks the candidate for his full name. Justin Hegarty responds clearly and politely, offering 'Justin' as his preferred form of address. When asked to show identification, the clip ends. Justin's delivery is measured and cooperative but notably brief; he speaks in short, complete sentences without elaboration or filler. Visually, he sits upright with hands clasped together on the desk, maintaining eye contact with the examiner. His affect appears calm but restrained, with minimal gesturing or expressive facial movement. The interaction is entirely formulaic, providing very little spontaneous behavioral data beyond the initial greeting and name exchange.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin keeps his hands tightly clasped on the desk throughout the brief exchange, with no visible fidgeting or gesture variation, while maintaining steady but somewhat fixed eye contact.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the clasped-hands posture reflects general test anxiety, a self-soothing control strategy, or simply a neutral resting position. Closer inspection of later segments (Part 2 monologue, Part 3 discussion) would reveal whether the posture persists under higher cognitive load or relaxes as familiarity grows, clarifying its diagnostic value for trait anxiety versus situational nervousness."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In the 20–40 s window, the camera remains fixed on the examiner, who methodically reads from his printed script while transitioning into Part 1 of the IELTS Speaking test. He asks Justin where he is from, and Justin's only audible contribution in this segment is a brief, trailing \"Uh…\" as he begins to formulate an answer. No visual data on Justin's posture, gaze, or gestures is available in this window because the framing excludes him entirely. The segment thus adds little new behavioral evidence about the interviewee beyond confirming the formal, scripted structure of the examination environment already implied by the earlier identification-check exchange.",
  "candidates": []
}

{
  "summary": "The source window [40.0, 60.0] contains no available interview segments. The provided transcript and video frames cover only the initial exchange (approximately 0–19 seconds), in which the interviewee, Justin, introduces himself as a student from Dunkirk, France, pursuing a Bachelor's of Science in Computers. No audio or visual data exists for the 40–60 second range, so no behavioral, vocal, or contextual observations can be drawn from this window.",
  "candidates": []
}

{
  "summary": "In this 20-second exchange, the interviewer asks Justin how he typically contacts friends and whether he prefers phone or email. Justin explains he uses his mobile phone to call friends, resorting to texting only when a conversation is urgent and a call isn't possible. When asked to choose between phone and email, he responds immediately and emphatically: 'Oh, definitely phone.' Throughout, his hands remain clasped on the desk with minimal gestural variation, and his gaze stays directed toward the interviewer with a somewhat fixed quality. His speech during the explanation contains fillers ('um,' 'so,' 'yeah') and a slight repetition ('I I have'), yet the final preference statement is delivered rapidly and with clear vocal emphasis, showing no hesitation.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's explanatory speech includes fillers and a mild stutter ('I I have'), but his concluding preference ('Oh, definitely phone') is delivered instantly, loudly, and without any hedging—creating a sharp contrast between the two phases of the same turn.",
      "goal": "Determine whether this hesitation-then-certainty pattern reflects a genuine deliberative processing style (thinking aloud before committing) or a social-performance strategy (softening the opening to appear thoughtful, then asserting confidently to signal decisiveness). Comparing this turn structure against later Part 3 discussion responses would clarify whether the pattern is a stable trait in his communication or situational to the low-stakes IELTS format."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 20-second window, Justin elaborates on his preference for phone over email communication, emphasizing the ability to hear a voice and gauge emotional state. His delivery is conversational but heavily hedged ('kind of,' 'you know,' 'in my opinion,' 'um'), and his hands make small, contained gestures on the desk surface rather than expansive movements. Around the 15-second mark, the camera briefly cuts to the interviewer—a man in glasses and a light shirt—who leans forward slightly while asking the follow-up 'And why is that?' Justin's response immediately after this prompt breaks fluency: he stutters ('I I really think') and his explanation is truncated mid-sentence ('you can't see…'), leaving the justification incomplete. The overall impression is of someone comfortable stating a preference but less so when pressed to articulate the underlying reasoning in real time.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's speech flows with hedges but remains relatively continuous while stating his preference; however, the instant the interviewer follows up with 'And why is that?', his fluency collapses into a stutter ('I I') and the sentence is cut off before completion, with his hands momentarily stilling on the desk.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the abrupt loss of fluency after a single probing question reflects a genuine difficulty constructing justifications on the spot (suggesting lower verbal-reasoning confidence under mild pressure) or a performance-aware pause where he is selecting words carefully. Comparing this micro-break against Part 3 responses—where the examiner typically issues multiple rapid follow-ups—would reveal whether the stutter is a one-off processing hiccup or a reliable marker of discomfort when his stated preferences are challenged."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 20-second window, Justin transitions from a hesitant, filler-laden reflection on phone communication ('you can't see their face, um, it's definitely just not the same. I think I'd probably say…') to an eager, structured response when the examiner asks what he dislikes about mobile phones. He immediately triple-affirms ('Yeah, yes, definitely'), then organizes his answer with 'first off' and launches into a vivid concrete scenario—being on a train in public and overhearing someone else's phone conversation. Visually, his hands shift from a tightly clasped resting position during the abstract comparison to small outward gestures as he narrates the train scene, and his speech cadence becomes more rhythmic and less interrupted once he anchors to a specific example.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's hands remain clasped and still while he speaks in abstract, hedged terms about phone-vs-face-to-face communication, but begin making small illustrative gestures the moment he shifts to the concrete 'I'm on the train, you can hear someone else' scenario—coinciding with his speech becoming faster and filler-free.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the gesture onset is tightly coupled to concrete-example activation (suggesting Justin's motor engagement tracks narrative specificity rather than emotional arousal) or whether it reflects a general comfort ramp that would also appear in later Part 3 abstract discussions. Comparing gesture timing against Part 3 segments where he must reason hypothetically without personal anecdotes would clarify whether his physical expressiveness is example-dependent or confidence-dependent."
    },
    {
      "hint": "Upon hearing 'is there anything you dislike about mobile phones?', Justin responds with a rapid triple affirmation ('Yeah, yes, definitely') before any reflective pause, then immediately structures with 'first off'—skipping the hedging and fillers that characterized his preceding turn.",
      "goal": "Assess whether the triple affirmation and instant structuring reflect genuine pre-formed opinions he is eager to voice (high certainty, low need for social calibration) or a performance strategy to signal cooperativeness and fill silence quickly. Tracking whether similar 'over-affirmation then list' patterns recur when the examiner asks open evaluative questions in Part 3 would distinguish a stable agreeable-compliance style from situational nervousness about appearing unprepared."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 20-second window, Justin delivers an emphatic, animated complaint about people using speakerphone in public ('the most annoying thing ever'), using expressive hand gestures and a raised, opinionated tone. However, the turn quickly deflates: he trails off with 'I don't know' and a visible uncertainty in his expression, unable to fully articulate his reasoning. The interviewer acknowledges briefly ('It's true') and transitions to a new topic (traveling) by reading from notes. Justin's response to 'Do you enjoy traveling to different places?' is a flat, minimal 'Sure'—a stark contrast to the energetic delivery of his preceding complaint. The segment captures a clear emotional asymmetry: high-intensity negative expression followed by low-engagement positive/neutral acknowledgment.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's speakerphone complaint is delivered with raised volume, forward lean, and active hand gestures, but collapses into 'I don't know' with a deflated posture; immediately after, his response to the travel question ('Sure') is flat, brief, and accompanied by a return to a neutral seated position with minimal gesturing.",
      "goal": "Determine whether this energy asymmetry—passionate negative expression versus subdued positive/neutral response—reflects a stable communicative trait (e.g., negativity bias in emotional expressiveness, or greater comfort critiquing than endorsing) or is situational (the travel question is broad and he simply lacks a prepared opinion, whereas the speakerphone irritation is a concrete, well-rehearsed grievance). Comparing his engagement level across Part 3 questions that ask him to evaluate positive experiences versus negative ones would clarify whether the pattern is valence-driven or topic-familiarity-driven."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 20-second segment (140–160 s), Justin discusses his love of travel with notable emotional animation. He opens emphatically ('Oh, absolutely. I love traveling to new countries'), makes a bold claim about having visited nearly every part of the UK, then pivots to a pragmatic explanation of his constraints ('The only reason I don't travel as much… is just because I don't have as much time and money, you know'). He closes by reaffirming his enthusiasm ('But I love it'). Visually, his posture remains seated with hands clasped on the desk, but his facial expression and forward lean convey genuine engagement with the topic. His speech contains natural fillers ('um', 'you know') and a slight hedge after the confident UK claim ('I probably been there'), yet overall fluency is high and the pacing accelerates during the enthusiastic portions and slows during the constraint explanation. The segment presents a clear affective arc: excitement → grounded limitation → reasserted passion.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin structures his travel response as an emotional 'sandwich': strong positive opening ('absolutely, I love'), a matter-of-fact middle acknowledging time/money limits (delivered slower, with the social-softening filler 'you know'), then an immediate return to enthusiasm ('But I love it'). His hands stay clasped throughout, but his brow lifts and he leans forward on the bookend phrases while his gaze drops briefly during the constraint sentence.",
      "goal": "Determine whether this positivity-framing-around-a-negative pattern is a stable communicative habit (always cushioning limitations within enthusiasm to project optimism) or a topic-specific response driven by genuine love of travel overriding practical frustration. Tracking whether similar 'sandwich' structures appear in Part 3 when he must discuss disadvantages of technology, work, or education would clarify whether the pattern reflects a trait-level emotional-regulation strategy (reframing negatives positively) or merely content-dependent enthusiasm. If the structure recurs even on topics he clearly dislikes, it suggests performative optimism; if it appears only when he has authentic passion, it signals genuine affective resilience."
    },
    {
      "hint": "When admitting he lacks 'time and money' for travel, Justin inserts 'you know' mid-sentence and his speaking rate drops noticeably compared to the surrounding enthusiastic clauses. The phrase 'as I feel like I would need to do that' carries a self-referential hedge ('I feel like') rather than a direct statement ('I need'), softening the admission of personal limitation.",
      "goal": "Assess whether the 'you know' + hedged phrasing around financial/temporal constraints reflects discomfort with self-disclosure (a vulnerability-management strategy to avoid sounding pitiful or to invite listener sympathy) or simply a natural conversational filler unrelated to the content's emotional weight. Comparing his language when discussing other potentially sensitive limitations (e.g., skills gaps, career uncertainties in Part 3) would reveal whether he systematically hedges around personal shortcomings—suggesting moderate self-consciousness about perceived inadequacy—or whether this instance is idiosyncratic to the travel topic where he feels he 'should' be traveling more."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "Justin opens by expressing enjoyment of meeting new people and seeing new faces, delivered in a casual, trailing tone ('things like that, you know'). When the interviewer asks which UK places he has visited, he produces a brief 'uh' filler, then enumerates three cities—London, Birmingham, Manchester—in a rhythmic, list-like cadence, adding the contextual detail 'with my friends during the holidays.' He then identifies London Bridge as 'probably my favorite' and the Prehistoric Museum as 'probably next,' using doubled hedging and a slightly slower, more drawn-out delivery than the city listing. Visually, his hands make small counting or tapping gestures during the enumeration, then settle as he shifts to the preference statement, with a modest forward lean and raised brow on 'London Bridge.'",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's city enumeration ('London, Birmingham, Manchester') is delivered in a flat, rhythmic, almost metronomic cadence with small finger-tapping gestures, while his preference statement ('London Bridge is probably my favorite… probably next would be the Prehistoric Museum') slows markedly, doubles the hedge 'probably,' and introduces a slight forward lean and brow raise—shifting from mechanical recall to tentative evaluation within the same turn.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the doubled 'probably' hedging and the slowdown from listing to preference reflect genuine real-time preference construction (he is deciding what he likes as he speaks, indicating authentic but unpolished introspection) or a social-calibration strategy to avoid sounding overly definitive to the examiner (protecting against a follow-up challenge). Comparing his evaluative language in Part 3—where the examiner typically presses 'Why?' or 'Do you agree?'—would reveal whether he systematically hedges preferences to maintain conversational flexibility or whether this instance is specific to the low-stakes listing format."
    },
    {
      "hint": "The initial 'uh' filler before the city list, followed by the parenthetical social context ('with my friends during the holidays'), suggests Justin is not simply retrieving facts but is also managing the social framing of his answer—ensuring the examiner understands these were group leisure trips rather than solo explorations. His gaze briefly drops during the 'uh' and the friend-context clause, then returns to the examiner for the preference statement.",
      "goal": "Assess whether the added social qualifier ('with my friends during the holidays') reflects a genuine need to contextualize his experience for the listener (high audience-awareness, cooperative communication style) or a subtle self-protective framing that distances him from claiming independent cultural exploration (managing impression of sophistication). Tracking whether he similarly adds relational qualifiers when discussing other activities in Part 3 (e.g., 'I usually go with…', 'my friends and I tend to…') would clarify whether this is a stable interpersonal-orientation trait or a one-off accommodation to the IELTS prompt's implicit expectation of individual travel narratives."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "The examiner transitions to Justin's hometown in France and asks whether it is a good place for young people to live. Justin responds immediately and emphatically ('Oh yeah, absolutely') with a visible smile and nod, hands still clasped. When the examiner follows up with 'Why?', Justin again opens with a doubled affirmation ('Oh yeah, children, absolutely') before launching into substantive content ('There's a bunch of open fields and parks for…'), at which point his hands unclasp and begin small illustrative gestures. Notably, the question referenced 'young people' broadly, but Justin's answer pivots specifically to 'children,' narrowing the demographic frame before offering the park/field justification. His delivery throughout is fast, loud, and free of fillers or hesitations, contrasting sharply with the hedged, stutter-prone responses observed in earlier segments about mobile phones and travel.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "When asked about 'young people,' Justin's first word of justification is 'children'—a narrower demographic term—delivered with the same emphatic 'Oh yeah, absolutely' framing he used for the yes/no answer. His hands remain clasped during both affirmations but activate into small open-palm gestures only when he reaches the concrete 'open fields and parks' content. The entire exchange from question to first substantive noun takes roughly 3 seconds with zero pause, filler, or self-correction.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the 'young people' → 'children' pivot reflects a genuine mental-model shortcut (he conceptualizes his hometown's appeal primarily through a family/childhood lens, so 'young people' automatically maps to 'children' in his schema) or a slight inattentiveness to the question's broader scope (answering a narrower version than what was asked). Comparing this against Part 3 responses—where questions often target abstract demographics (e.g., 'the elderly,' 'working adults,' 'teenagers')—would reveal whether he systematically reframes demographic terms into his own preferred categories (suggesting a concrete, experience-grounded cognition style) or whether this was a one-off slip under the low-pressure Part 1 format."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "Justin evaluates his hometown's suitability across age demographics in a single comparative turn. For teenagers, his delivery is hedged and tentative ('I'd probably say not so much just because there's not as much facilities'), listing movie theaters, gyms, and cafes as missing infrastructure. For children, his tone shifts sharply to emphatic certainty, but with a distinctive self-narrowing refinement: 'definitely children, yes, younger children, definitely.' His entire justification framework is material and infrastructure-based (open fields, parks vs. built amenities), with no reference to social dynamics, safety, or cultural factors. Visually, he looks down at his papers while constructing the comparison, then lifts his gaze for the emphatic child conclusion. The segment closes with the interviewer's soft 'Mm' acknowledgment—signaling the answer landed as complete—followed by a transition question about whether the hometown has changed over the years. The camera cuts to the interviewer reviewing notes, creating a brief structural pause before the next topic.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's certainty for children follows a narrow-then-intensify arc: he first says 'definitely children,' affirms with 'yes,' then SEMANTICALLY RESTRICTS to 'younger children' while RE-EMPHASIZING with 'definitely'—a pattern distinct from the flat triple repetition ('Yeah, yes, definitely') seen earlier. His hands, previously clasped and still during the teenager hedge, appear to make a small forward gesture or finger-tap coinciding with the 'younger children' refinement, and his brow lifts on the final 'definitely.'",
      "goal": "Determine whether the simultaneous narrowing-and-intensifying pattern reflects genuine epistemic refinement (he recognized 'children' was too broad, corrected to 'younger children,' and the precision boost made him MORE confident) or a defensive-communication strategy (restricting scope makes the claim easier to justify, so he leans harder into certainty to compensate for the concession). Tracking whether similar 'narrow then double-down' sequences recur when he self-corrects in Part 3 abstract discussions—where scope restriction isn't possible—would clarify whether this is a stable argumentative habit or a topic-specific accommodation to the concrete hometown question."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "Justin responds to a prompt about Dunkirk's historical character with a contemplative opening ('Hmm… It's an interesting question') before committing to a confident, detail-rich explanation: the town was left in ruins after WWII, and old churches and shipwrecks remain visible to this day. Once past the initial hesitation, his speech flows with only minor fillers ('Um,' a trailing 'Uh…'), and his vocabulary shifts to concrete spatial nouns ('ruins,' 'old churches,' 'shipwrecks,' 'still standing'). Visually, his hands remain clasped and still during the deliberative opening but transition into small illustrative gestures as he enumerates the physical remnants, with slight forward lean and engaged brow. The segment ends mid-thought on 'Uh…', suggesting the answer was cut short.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's response follows a clear two-phase arc: Phase 1 (0–4 s) is marked by the vocalized 'Hmm,' the social acknowledgment 'It's an interesting question,' and a still clasped-hands posture with fixed gaze; Phase 2 (4–20 s) begins with 'Yeah, Dunkirk is a very historic town' delivered at higher volume and faster rate, accompanied by progressive hand unclasping into small open-palm gestures timed to the concrete nouns 'ruins,' 'churches,' and 'shipwrecks.' The trailing 'Uh…' at ~19 s indicates an unfinished thought.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the Phase 1→Phase 2 transition reflects genuine real-time construction of a historically grounded answer (i.e., he needed the 'Hmm' to retrieve and organize WWII-era facts about Dunkirk) or a low-effort social ritual applied to a topic he knows well, with the gestural activation being a reflexive response to spatial/concrete imagery rather than a sign of deep engagement. Comparing this pattern against Part 3 abstract-evaluation responses—where no concrete visual referents exist to trigger gestures—would reveal whether his illustrative hand movements are content-driven (tied to spatial nouns) or arousal-driven (tied to confidence level), clarifying whether the initial hesitation is epistemic or merely performative."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "Justin describes his hometown as having been significantly industrialized, noting modern houses and increased factory presence, delivered in a confident declarative tone. When the examiner asks about local occupations, he quickly answers that many residents are farmers who grow vegetables like potatoes over the summer, though this second part is punctuated by fillers ('um,' 'you know') and the sentence is cut off mid-word ('potato-'). His posture remains relatively neutral and seated throughout, with hands resting on the desk area, and his gaze stays directed toward the examiner during both statements. The overall register is descriptive and matter-of-fact rather than evaluative or emotionally charged.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin's industrialization statement ('it's definitely industrialized a lot') is delivered in a single confident breath with no fillers, yet his immediately following occupational description ('a lot of them are farmers, um, they'll grow, you know, a lot of vegetables') introduces two fillers and a slower pace within the same turn—suggesting the structural change is an established belief while the livelihood details require on-the-spot retrieval or carry less personal certainty.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the filler asymmetry within this single turn reflects a genuine difference in epistemic confidence (he knows the town changed but is less certain about specific occupational breakdowns) or is simply his default rhythm when enumerating examples versus making a summary judgment. Comparing this intra-turn contrast against Part 3 responses where he must generate abstract justifications would clarify whether fillers cluster around listing/retrieval tasks regardless of topic familiarity, or specifically around content he feels less authoritative about."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "This segment captures the closing seconds of Justin's Part 1 response about agricultural work in his hometown. He enumerates concrete details—potatoes, farm stands, selling produce—with a single 'um' filler and a notable lexical repetition ('a lot, a lot of them are farmers'), accompanied by small illustrative hand gestures visible in the frames. His tone is descriptive and matter-of-fact rather than hesitant. The examiner then interjects smoothly with 'Let's see. Now moving on to the second part of the speaking test,' signaling the transition from the low-stakes Part 1 Q&A to the more demanding Part 2 monologue. Justin's posture and expression appear neutral-to-slightly-relaxed as his answer concludes, with no visible tension or eagerness to continue.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "Justin repeats 'a lot' twice in quick succession ('So a lot, a lot of them are farmers') while delivering what functions as a summarizing conclusion to his occupational description. The repetition is not accompanied by a pause, pitch drop, or self-correction marker (e.g., 'I mean'), and his hand gesture remains a small open-palm motion rather than tightening or stopping—suggesting the repetition serves a rhetorical/emphatic purpose rather than signaling retrieval difficulty.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the 'a lot, a lot' doubling is a stable discourse-level summarizing device (equivalent to 'so essentially' or 'in short') that he deploys when wrapping up descriptive answers, or whether it occasionally masks residual uncertainty about the proportion he is claiming. Comparing this instance against Part 2 and Part 3 conclusions—where he must synthesize longer arguments—would reveal whether he systematically uses reduplicated quantifiers as a closing cadence (a stylistic habit) or only when he feels the preceding evidence is thin and needs reinforcement (an epistemic hedge)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "This is a text-only transcript of Justin Hegarthy's complete IELTS Speaking Part 1 (≈269 s). He identifies as a BSc Computer Science student from Dunkirk, France, and answers questions on communication habits, mobile-phone dislikes, travel, UK cities, his hometown's suitability for young people, historical change, and local occupations. Across all topics he displays a highly consistent discourse architecture: (1) near-universal affirmative openers ('Yeah,' 'Oh yeah absolutely'); (2) dense hedging clusters ('you know,' 'kind of,' 'things like that,' 'I'd probably say') that intensify around abstract or evaluative content and recede around concrete personal anecdotes; (3) an emotional-sandwich pattern in which every stated limitation (time/money for travel, lack of facilities for teenagers) is bracketed by enthusiastic endorsement; (4) self-narrowing corrections ('children' → 'younger children') that tighten scope while re-asserting certainty; (5) a single rhetorical escalation—the speakerphone grievance—which is the longest, most exclamatory, and least hedged passage in the entire interview; and (6) inclusive rapport markers ('you know what I mean,' 'I'll tell you that') that position the examiner as a sympathetic listener. Fluency, specificity, and energy rise sharply when he accesses lived sensory experience (hearing someone on a train, pointing out spots on a UK map, open fields for kids) and flatten when he must evaluate abstract categories or retrieve occupational statistics. The text reveals a cooperative, socially calibrated speaker who systematically avoids bare negatives, qualifies every constraint with a positive bookend, and uses hedging as a face-management tool rather than a sign of genuine uncertainty.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "In the text transcript, Justin never issues an unqualified negative or bare limitation. Every constraint is linguistically sandwiched: travel limitation ('I don't have as much time and money') is immediately followed by 'but I love it'; teenage facilities deficit ('not as much facilities') is preceded by 'Oh yeah children absolutely' and followed by specific positives for children; even the speakerphone grievance ends with the softening aside 'I don't know it's true.' The hedging lexicon ('you know,' 'kind of,' 'things like that,' 'I'd probably say') appears 11+ times and clusters tightly around evaluative or limiting statements, while concrete descriptive passages (city list, Dunkirk ruins, farmer vegetables) contain zero such hedges.",
      "goal": "Determine whether this systematic sandwich-and-hedge architecture reflects a stable interpersonal trait—high agreeableness combined with conflict-avoidance and a default strategy of protecting both his own and the interlocutor's face—or is a situational artifact of the IELTS format, where he perceives the examiner as an authority figure whose approval he must not jeopardize. Text-only analysis allows exact frequency counts of hedge tokens per topic-type (evaluative vs. descriptive vs. narrative) without prosodic confounds, and comparison against Part 2/Part 3 transcripts (where the examiner's authority framing is identical but the cognitive demand differs) would reveal whether the sandwich pattern is topic-triggered (only around limitations/disagreements) or a global discourse default that persists even when he has no reason to soften his stance."
    }
  ]
}
